[38;5;252m[1m## Behaviours I confirmed the Rust replicates rather than "fixes"[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C's [38;5;10mstrchr(ignore, c)[0m matches its own NUL terminator, so an embedded [38;5;10m0x00[0m is treated as ignorable at even nibbles whenever [38;5;10mignore[0m is non-NULL — even when [38;5;10mignore[0m is [38;5;10m""[0m. The [38;5;10mhex_pos--[0m on a trailing unpaired nibble makes [38;5;10m*hex_end_p[0m point [3mat[23m that digit. The [38;5;10mbin_pos >= bin_maxlen[0m check runs for high nibbles too, so [38;5;10m2*bin_maxlen + 1[0m valid digits fails rather than truncating. And [38;5;10mc_num0[0m/[38;5;10mc_alpha0[0m depend on [38;5;10munsigned int[0m promotion — computing them in [38;5;10mu8[0m breaks them, which mutants M2/M3 confirm.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Two caveats worth stating[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mif (ret != 0) bin_pos = 0;[0m (L47) is unobservable: the function returns [38;5;10mret[0m on that path, so no test can distinguish it. I could not verify it and no test claims to.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Mutant M5 — deferring the buffer-full check from the high to the low nibble — survives the entire suite, including an exhaustive sweep of all inputs up to 6 bytes and all 65 536 two-byte inputs. That is not a gap: it is semantically equivalent, because [38;5;10mbin_pos[0m cannot change between the two check points, and if the error fires one iteration later, [38;5;10mstate[0m is now [38;5;10m0xFF[0m so the epilogue's [38;5;10mhex_pos--[0m lands on exactly the index the C reports. I encoded it as an equivalence [3massertion[23m (the control now fails if it ever gets killed) rather than leaving it as an apparent blind spot.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
